# 02 — Serial console access

The F800 has no usable video output at boot. The BIOS, the OPNsense installer and the first boot all happen over the **serial console**. Get this working before anything else — nothing in the rest of this guide is possible without it.

## What you need

| Item | Notes |
|---|---|
| Console cable | RJ45
| USB-to-serial adapter | anything, i use CH340 but i need to use linux
| Terminal software | PuTTY (Windows), `screen` or `minicom` (Linux/macOS) |

Locate the console port on the chassis. in front of the firewall  

![Console port on the chassis](assets/console-port.jpg)

## Terminal settings

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Baud rate | 19200 ( for the barracuda, 115200 for opnsense) |
| Data bits | 8 |
| Parity | None |
| Stop bits | 1 |
| Flow control | None |

> This table is the single most searched-for piece of information about this appliance. Replace the placeholder with the value you actually observed — not a guess.

Shorthand for `8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit` is **8N1**.

You can modify the baud rate in the BIOS. ( tip : set it to 115200 for to match the opnsense default)

## Connecting

### Windows (PuTTY)

1. Plug in the USB-to-serial adapter and open **Device Manager**.
2. Expand **Ports (COM & LPT)** and note the port number, e.g. `COM3`.
   If the device shows a yellow warning triangle, the driver is missing — install the one matching your adapter's chipset.
3. Open PuTTY, select **Serial** as connection type.
4. Enter your COM port in **Serial line** and the baud rate in **Speed**.
5. Click **Open**.

![PuTTY serial settings](assets/putty-serial.png)

### Linux / macOS

```bash
# Identify the device
ls /dev/ttyUSB* /dev/ttyS* 2>/dev/null      # Linux
ls /dev/tty.usbserial* /dev/tty.usbmodem*   # macOS

# Connect (adjust device and baud rate)
screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200
```

To exit `screen`: `Ctrl+A`, then `K`, then `y`.

If you get a permission error on Linux, add yourself to the `dialout` group and log back in:

```bash
sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER
```

An alternative some people find friendlier:

```bash
minicom -D /dev/ttyUSB0 -b 115200
```

